ArchiveAscii: write a byte and a word with the `int` type token, as ZenGin does.

ASCII archive entries carry a type token that the reader checks, and
`WriteArchiveAscii` spelled two of them in a vocabulary nothing reads.
`write_byte` emitted `name=byte:200` and `write_word` `name=word:...`, but
`ReadArchiveAscii::read_byte` and `::read_word` both call `read_entry("int")`
and throw `type mismatch: expected int, got: byte` on anything else. The
archive was therefore unreadable by its own reader at the first byte or word
entry it contained.

ZenGin settles which side is wrong. Across the 24 `zCArchiverGeneric` worlds of
a retail Gothic II install:

    =int:       144111
    =byte:           0
    =word:           0

and `OldCamp.zen` writes the field this surfaced on as
`decalAlphaWeight=int:200`. The reader is right; the writer was inventing
tokens.

The practical effect was total: `decalAlphaWeight` (`VisualDecal::save`) is the
only `write_byte` call reachable outside a savegame, and 19 of the install's 20
ASCII worlds carry at least one decal — so every one of them failed to re-load
after a re-save, at the first decal in the file. Fixing it is what takes the
ASCII round-trip from "loads back" (patch 0024) to "loads back completely".

Same defect class as 0004 (`alphaFunc` written bool, read enum) and A3
(`write_mat3x3` writes `rawFloat:` where the reader wants `raw:`), which is
still open.

--- a/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
+++ b/src/archive/ArchiveAscii.cc
@@ -312,14 +312,19 @@
 		this->write_entry(name, "float", std::to_string(v));
 	}
 
+	// The type token is `int`, not `byte`/`word`. ReadArchiveAscii::read_byte
+	// and ::read_word both call read_entry("int"), and ZenGin agrees: across the
+	// 24 ASCII worlds of a retail Gothic II install there are 144,111 `int:`
+	// entries and not one `byte:` or `word:`. Writing the narrower token made
+	// the archive unreadable by this very reader at the first such entry.
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_byte(std::string_view name, std::uint8_t v) {
 		std::array<char, std::numeric_limits<uint8_t>::digits10 + 1> buf {};
-		this->write_entry(name, "byte", intosv(buf, v));
+		this->write_entry(name, "int", intosv(buf, v));
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_word(std::string_view name, std::uint16_t v) {
 		std::array<char, std::numeric_limits<uint16_t>::digits10 + 1> buf {};
-		this->write_entry(name, "word", intosv(buf, v));
+		this->write_entry(name, "int", intosv(buf, v));
 	}
 
 	void WriteArchiveAscii::write_enum(std::string_view name, std::uint32_t v) {
